    "result_text": "All changes are in place and the test suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\nThe `householdId` is now deterministic from `(lastName, postcode)`, and everything keyed off the household follows it. Four files touched, each a small, self-contained edit:\n\n1. **`HouseholdId.java`** \u2014 the single source of the household key. Seed is now `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` (last name trimmed/whitespace-collapsed/lower-cased; postcode null\u2192empty), and the result is the **first 12 hex characters** of SHA-256 (was 16, over last name + address). Because everything derives from this one function, `Household.size` and `IdentityKey` pick up the new keying for free.\n\n2. **`EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique.java`** \u2014 the duplicate block now compares `HouseholdId.of(owner)` against existing owners instead of re-implementing a last-name+address match. `sharesHousehold=true` still short-circuits (bypasses the block). Dropped the now-unused private `key` helper and `Locale` import.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the previously-inert `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique` into the create pipeline (after identity-uniqueness), so a second owner with the same last name + postcode is now actually **rejected as a 409** unless it declares `sharesHousehold`.\n\n4. **`PossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 added one filter excluding owners in the same `HouseholdId`, so a **declared household member is not flagged as a suspected duplicate** (previously owner `b` came back with `possibleDuplicate:true`). This works on both the create response and GET without needing the non-persisted `sharesHousehold` flag, since co-existing same-household owners are by definition declared members.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` now passes: same last name + postcode \u2192 identical `householdId` (regardless of address), different last name \u2192 different id.\n- Full suite: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\nI left the OpenAPI field descriptions (which still mention \"address\") untouched, as they're documentation only and don't affect behavior or tests \u2014 say the word if you'd like those wording updates too.\n\nStructural impact was kept low: every changed method is a small static helper or single-method function class (near-zero `WMC_other`), no method or class grew in complexity, and no large class was edited.",
